            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has is reasonably air tight, and the key air leakage areas are associated with the intentional openings for the current oil boiler - the 6" chimney and the 6" open combustion air pipe. If you were to upgrade to a direct vent boiler, the current chimney &amp; combustion air pipe would be eliminated. 



There is some air leakage from each of the 4 bathroom exhaust fans. If you decide to replace these, please choose ENERGY STAR® rated models that use very little electricity, have great dampers to reduce air leakage &amp; they operate at very low sounds levels. You may even want to install timer switches to control these exhaust fans so they are not accidentally left on for extended periods, pulling the heated air from your house.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 117&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <VentilationSystem>

HRV



During my assessment, your house still has the original Flair Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) unit operational. There were some "recall notices" on some of these Flair ventilation units, however I do not believe your model 45300 was on that list. In addition to the HRV, there are many exhaust fans for targeted "spot ventilation" - 4 bathroom fans, the down-draft range in the kitchen, the humidistat controlled high wall exhaust fan in the kitchen, and the dryer.



Overall, you will notice if your house is under-ventilated if stale air &amp; odors linger within the house, or there is excess humidity during the winter and condensation continuously forms on your windows. If your house is over-ventilated, the house will feel quite dry and the excess ventilation represents unnecessary heat loss. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



Overall, it appears your house is relatively air tight and the HRV was operating continuously upon my arrival. Based on the age of your current HRV, I would recommend that you have a new ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. A new ENERGY STAR® rated HRV will have a much higher heat recovery efficiency rating and use significantly less electricity. The central controls for the HRV should have more options for air exchange &amp; air circulation program cycles.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 50&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>



&lt;b&gt;The upgrade recommendation for your domestic hot water is integrated into the boiler upgrade recommendation above.&lt;/b&gt;







</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil-fired boiler is rated at 85% AFUE, and vents up a 6" flue. This boiler is used to heat all areas of your house (including the garage - which is outside of our analyses) and the boiler also heats your domestic hot water with the integrated Amitrol 60 USG storage tank. There is a 6" open combustion air supply for the boiler.



During the assessment, we talked about higher efficiency direct vent systems (oil or propane), where the chimney could be eliminated and the heating system(s) would no longer rely on internal house air for combustion.



As discussed, the higher efficiency ENERGY STAR® direct vent oil boilers are rated about 87% AFUE - only about 2% higher than your current boiler. I therefore modeled an ENERGY STAR® direct vent propane boiler with a 95% AFUE. This boiler would be direct vented out through the basement header space, and would therefore eliminate the current 6" chimney and the 6" open combustion air supply - providing air leakage reduction savings. When these boilers are used to also heat your hot water (including the existing Amitrol storage tank), the hot water heating efficiency increases by about 40%. I have modeled the savings associated with this design.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with an ENERGY STAR® direct vent propane boilerr with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, and using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (with the Amitrol Storage tank), there are currently no annual energy savings (as the price of oil has dropped so much over the past few years), however your annual energy usage would decrease by &lt;b&gt;52.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system near the front of the upper right-facing roof (orientation = S), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 969&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has 4 toilets and 3 of them are high consumption 13 litre models. The only one that is a lower water consumption unit (on the main floor) is one that you indicate does not work very well. I would therefore recommend you replace all 4 toilets. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range down draft fan, 4 bathroom fans, the kitchen exhaust on a humidistat, and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.7 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliances (the boiler). The pellet stove &amp; propane fireplace appear to be sealed combustion, direct-vent units.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



